<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ovid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/43-bce">43 BCE</a>–c. 17 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Publius Ovidius Naso</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ovid was a Roman poet, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulmona">Sulmo</a> in the land of the Paeligni in 43 BCE, the author of the Metamorphoses, the Art of Love and the Fasti, a poem on the Roman calendar and its festivals. In 8 CE Augustus banished him to Tomis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, where he wrote the Tristia and the Letters from Pontus, lamenting the cold and his barbarian neighbours, and he died there around 17 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ovid.jpg" alt="Ovid" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucasaw · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ovid_by_an_anonymous_sculptor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through Roman poets such as <b>Ovid</b>, its gods and heroes passed into medieval and Renaissance literature and art, from Botticelli’s Birth of Venus to the operas, poems and novels of modern times, and its stories remain among the most widely known in the world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ovid</b>’s Fasti, a poem on the festivals from January to June, mixes antiquarian lore, popular custom and poetic invention.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Virgil’s Aeneid gave Rome a national epic, Horace wrote odes and satires, <b>Ovid</b> retold the myths of transformation in the Metamorphoses, and Livy wrote a history of Rome from its foundation.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Without access to Homer, Dante took Virgil, Lucan, <b>Ovid</b> and Statius as models, Virgil above all.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Brentano drew on <b>Ovid</b> and the myth of Echo.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a></li><li><blockquote>Falerii was famous for the cult of Juno Curitis, whose festival <b>Ovid</b> described, and for its temples decorated with fine terracottas.</blockquote><a href="/state/falerii">Falerii</a></li></ul></section>
